<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>How many Czech brand templates are there? Three.</title>
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
<style>{{ inline_css }}</style>
</head>
<body>
<main>

<header class="masthead">
    <p class="eyebrow">Brand Reflection Series &middot; Episode 2 &middot; companion to
        <a href="https://sandovabarbora.github.io/color-fingerprint-cz/" style="color:inherit;">Color fingerprint</a>
        &amp;
        <a href="https://sandovabarbora.github.io/sound-fingerprint-cz/" style="color:inherit;">Sound fingerprint</a>
    </p>
    <h1>Three templates, one institution.</h1>
    <p class="lede">{{ counts.n_brands_used }} Czech brand homepages embedded in a multilingual semantic space cluster into four neighborhoods — and one of those neighborhoods contains <em>all five banks, plus Generali, plus &#268;EZ, plus &#352;koda Auto</em>. Sector identity we treat as obvious at the campaign level dissolves at the homepage layer. This is the map, and the moves it suggests.</p>
    <p class="byline">By {{ author }}. <a href="{{ repo_url }}">Source on GitHub</a>.</p>
</header>

<section class="hero-stat">
    <p class="hero-stat-line">Eight brands. Four sectors. One homepage template.</p>
    <p class="hero-stat-cap">&#268;SOB, &#268;esk&aacute; spo&#345;itelna, Fio banka, Air Bank, Komer&#269;n&iacute; banka, Generali, &#268;EZ, &#352;koda Auto. The boundary between them, on the homepage layer, is zero.</p>
</section>

<section class="method">
    <p class="method-label">Method</p>
    <p class="method-body">Homepage text per brand (title, meta, first headings, first non-promo paragraphs, capped at 600 chars). Top rising Czech queries via pytrends with a hand-curated fallback. Both corpora embedded with <code>intfloat/multilingual-e5-base</code> (passage / query prefixes per model docs, L2-normalized). Layout via UMAP. Cluster labels rolled from sector composition. Opportunity score = velocity &times; (1 &minus; mean cosine-similarity to the 3 nearest brands), computed in the 768-dim embedding space, not on the 2D map.</p>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">01</span><h2>The map</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">Brands are filled circles colored by cluster. Rising queries are outlined diamonds sized by velocity. Distance on this map is an approximation of semantic distance in 768 dimensions; treat it as topology, not as a metric. The four cluster names are derived from sector composition, not chosen by the analyst.</p>
    <div class="map-wrap">
        <figure class="map-frame">
            {{ map_svg | safe }}
            <figcaption class="scatter-cap">UMAP layout. Click any neighborhood to inspect the brands and vocabulary that define it.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <aside id="cluster-panel" class="cluster-panel" aria-live="polite">
            <p class="cluster-panel-hint">Click a neighborhood on the map to see which brands share it and which words define it.</p>
        </aside>
    </div>
    <div class="legend-strip">
{% for chip in legend %}
        <div class="legend-chip">
            <span class="legend-dot" style="background: {{ chip.color }};"></span>
            <div class="legend-meta">
                <p class="legend-name">{{ chip.name }}</p>
                <p class="legend-brands">{{ chip.n_brands }} brands &mdash; {{ chip.brand_examples }}</p>
            </div>
        </div>
{% endfor %}
    </div>
</section>

<aside class="pull">
    <p class="pull-q">Czech banks, &#268;EZ and &#352;koda Auto cluster as <span class="num">one</span> institutional brand. Your brand is not where you think it is.</p>
    <p class="pull-cap">The boundary between &#268;SOB and &#268;EZ is invisible at the homepage layer. The boundary between Lidl and Avast is invisible at the homepage layer. The boundaries we name in pitch decks are campaign-level fictions that the homepage doesn't honor.</p>
</aside>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">02</span><h2>Where the rising trends fall</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">For each rising query, the three brands whose homepage text sits closest in the semantic space. Rows flagged <span class="low-conf-inline">low&#8209;signal</span> are queries whose top three brand matches are within 0.025 cosine of each other &mdash; the &quot;winner&quot; is essentially arbitrary and signals a sector gap in the corpus. Treat those as evidence of a corpus gap, not a recommended brand activation.</p>
    <table class="opp-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Trend</th>
                <th class="num-col">Velocity</th>
                <th>Nearest brands</th>
                <th class="num-col">Saturation</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
{% for o in opportunities %}
            <tr class="{% if o.low_confidence %}opp-low-conf{% endif %}">
                <td class="opp-trend">
                    {{ o.trend }}
                    {% if o.low_confidence %}<span class="low-conf-badge">low signal</span>{% endif %}
                </td>
                <td class="num-col">{{ "%.0f" | format(o.velocity) }}</td>
                <td class="opp-nearby">
                    {{ o.near_brand_1 }}<span class="opp-sim">({{ "%.2f" | format(o.near_brand_1_sim) }})</span>
                    {% if o.near_brand_2 %} &middot; {{ o.near_brand_2 }}<span class="opp-sim">({{ "%.2f" | format(o.near_brand_2_sim) }})</span>{% endif %}
                    {% if o.near_brand_3 %} &middot; {{ o.near_brand_3 }}<span class="opp-sim">({{ "%.2f" | format(o.near_brand_3_sim) }})</span>{% endif %}
                </td>
                <td class="num-col">{{ "%.2f" | format(o.saturation_score) }}</td>
            </tr>
{% endfor %}
        </tbody>
    </table>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">03</span><h2>Patterns</h2></div>
    <div class="patterns">
{% for p in patterns %}
        <article class="pattern">
            <p class="num">{{ "%02d" | format(loop.index) }}</p>
            <div>
                <h3>{{ p.title }}</h3>
                <p class="evidence">{{ p.evidence }}</p>
                <p class="takeaway"><strong>Why it matters</strong>{{ p.takeaway }}</p>
            </div>
        </article>
{% endfor %}
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">04</span><h2>Playbook</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">Five moves a strategist or creative director can run on next week's pitch using this map.</p>
    <div class="playbook">
{% for move in playbook %}
        <article class="play">
            <p class="play-num">{{ "%02d" | format(loop.index) }}</p>
            <div>
                <h3>{{ move.title }}</h3>
                <p class="play-body">{{ move.body }}</p>
            </div>
        </article>
{% endfor %}
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <div class="section-label"><span class="num">05</span><h2>What this looks like for one brand</h2></div>
    <p class="section-intro">A walk-through of the same diagnostic applied to Air Bank. The point isn&apos;t Air Bank specifically &mdash; it&apos;s the shape of the strategist&apos;s decision once the map exists. Substitute any brand on the map and the steps stay the same.</p>

    <div class="worked">
        <article class="worked-step">
            <p class="worked-num">01</p>
            <div>
                <h3>Where Air Bank sits on the map</h3>
                <p>Air Bank embeds inside the <strong>Banking + heavy industry</strong> neighborhood, next to &#268;SOB, &#268;esk&aacute; spo&#345;itelna, Fio banka, Komer&#269;n&iacute; banka, Generali, &#268;EZ, and &#352;koda Auto. Three banks plus an insurer plus an energy company plus a carmaker all read as one brand to the homepage layer.</p>
                <p class="worked-note">On the map, Air Bank tilts slightly toward the consumer side of the institutional neighborhood &mdash; closer to the boundary than its peers. That tilt is the only visible difference between Air Bank and Komer&#269;n&iacute;, and it&apos;s small.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="worked-step">
            <p class="worked-num">02</p>
            <div>
                <h3>The vocabulary it shares with seven other brands</h3>
                <p>The defining tokens for the institutional neighborhood, derived directly from homepage TF&#8209;IDF, are: <code>banka</code>, <code>&scaron;koda</code>, <code>&#269;ez</code>, <code>fio</code>, <code>&#269;esk&aacute;</code>. These are the words that distinguish this neighborhood from the other three on the map.</p>
                <p>If Air Bank&apos;s homepage shares those tokens with &#268;EZ&apos;s homepage, then to a buyer&apos;s first read, Air Bank <em>is</em> &#268;EZ. The challenger positioning &mdash; &quot;the bank that&apos;s different from Komer&#269;n&iacute;&quot; &mdash; is decided in the campaign layer and quietly undone in the homepage layer.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="worked-step">
            <p class="worked-num">03</p>
            <div>
                <h3>Which playbook moves apply, in order</h3>
                <p><strong>Move 02</strong> &mdash; list the institutional vocabulary explicitly in the brief and rule it out. Not as creative aspiration; as a word-level constraint. The first draft that lands with <code>banka</code>, <code>partnerem</code> or <code>silnej&scaron;&iacute;</code> on the homepage goes back.</p>
                <p><strong>Move 03</strong> &mdash; stop pitching against named banks. The boundary that matters is the one between the institutional neighborhood and the mass-market consumer neighborhood (Lidl, Albert, McDonald&apos;s, Kofola). The brand argument becomes &quot;we sit closer to those brands than to &#268;EZ.&quot; That&apos;s a positioning move with a measurable test.</p>
                <p><strong>Move 01</strong> &mdash; after the rebrand, re-score. If the homepage didn&apos;t move neighborhoods, neither did the brand. Soft success at the campaign layer with no movement at the homepage layer is the failure mode this episode is built to catch.</p>
            </div>
        </article>

        <article class="worked-step">
            <p class="worked-num">04</p>
            <div>
                <h3>What this would look like in a deck</h3>
                <p>One map slide showing Air Bank&apos;s current position, one arrow showing the intended trajectory toward the consumer neighborhood, one slide listing the vocabulary to drop and the vocabulary to adopt. The arrow is the strategic ask; the vocabulary lists are the executional spec. A brief that doesn&apos;t end with both lists isn&apos;t finished.</p>
            </div>
        </article>
    </div>
</section>

<footer>
    <div>
        <h4>Brand Reflection Series</h4>
        <p><a href="https://sandovabarbora.github.io/color-fingerprint-cz/">Episode 1 &mdash; Color fingerprint</a></p>
        <p><a href="https://sandovabarbora.github.io/sound-fingerprint-cz/">Episode 1.5 &mdash; Sound fingerprint</a></p>
        <p>Episode 2 (this) &mdash; Strategic topography</p>
        <p><a href="https://sandovabarbora.github.io/brand-reflection-cz/">Series hub + case studies</a></p>
        <p><a href="https://sandovabarbora.github.io/spot-scorer/">Spot Scorer &mdash; instant brief generator</a></p>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h4>Code</h4>
        <p><a href="{{ repo_url }}">strategic-topography-cz</a></p>
        <p>MIT for code, CC&#8209;BY&#8209;4.0 for derived data.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h4>Disclaimer</h4>
        <p>Analysis of publicly available brand communication. Brands are referenced for analytical purposes only. Positioning reflects current homepage messaging at time of scrape. No agency attribution is implied.</p>
    </div>
</footer>

</main>

<script id="cluster-panel-data" type="application/json">{{ cluster_panel_json | safe }}</script>
<script>
(function() {
    const dataEl = document.getElementById("cluster-panel-data");
    if (!dataEl) return;
    const clusters = JSON.parse(dataEl.textContent);
    const byId = new Map(clusters.map(c => [String(c.id), c]));
    const panel = document.getElementById("cluster-panel");
    const map = document.querySelector(".topo-map");
    if (!panel || !map) return;

    const groups = Array.from(map.querySelectorAll(".cluster-group"));

    function clear(node) { while (node.firstChild) node.removeChild(node.firstChild); }
    function el(tag, opts) {
        const node = document.createElement(tag);
        if (opts && opts.cls) node.className = opts.cls;
        if (opts && opts.text != null) node.textContent = opts.text;
        return node;
    }

    function renderPanel(c) {
        clear(panel);
        const header = el("div", { cls: "cluster-panel-head" });
        const dot = el("span", { cls: "cluster-panel-dot" });
        dot.style.background = c.color;
        header.appendChild(dot);
        header.appendChild(el("p", { cls: "cluster-panel-name", text: c.name }));
        panel.appendChild(header);

        panel.appendChild(el("p", { cls: "cluster-panel-meta", text: c.brands.length + " brands in this neighborhood" }));

        const brandsLabel = el("p", { cls: "cluster-panel-label", text: "Brands" });
        panel.appendChild(brandsLabel);
        const brandList = el("p", { cls: "cluster-panel-brands", text: c.brands.join(", ") });
        panel.appendChild(brandList);

        const tokensLabel = el("p", { cls: "cluster-panel-label", text: "Top TF-IDF tokens" });
        panel.appendChild(tokensLabel);
        const tokens = el("ul", { cls: "cluster-panel-tokens" });
        c.top_tokens.forEach(tok => {
            const li = el("li", { cls: "cluster-panel-token", text: tok });
            tokens.appendChild(li);
        });
        panel.appendChild(tokens);

        const closeBtn = el("button", { cls: "cluster-panel-close", text: "Clear selection" });
        closeBtn.type = "button";
        closeBtn.addEventListener("click", clearSelection);
        panel.appendChild(closeBtn);
    }

    function selectCluster(cid) {
        const c = byId.get(String(cid));
        if (!c) return;
        groups.forEach(g => {
            const active = g.getAttribute("data-cluster-id") === String(cid);
            g.classList.toggle("is-active", active);
            g.classList.toggle("is-dimmed", !active);
        });
        renderPanel(c);
    }

    function clearSelection() {
        groups.forEach(g => {
            g.classList.remove("is-active");
            g.classList.remove("is-dimmed");
        });
        clear(panel);
        panel.appendChild(el("p", { cls: "cluster-panel-hint", text: "Click a neighborhood on the map to see which brands share it and which words define it." }));
    }

    groups.forEach(g => {
        const cid = g.getAttribute("data-cluster-id");
        g.addEventListener("click", () => selectCluster(cid));
        g.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
            if (e.key === "Enter" || e.key === " ") {
                e.preventDefault();
                selectCluster(cid);
            }
        });
    });
})();
</script>

</body>
</html>
